The record corrections check out where the source can verify them: the added S2 transfers precede `live`, the ACCEPT contract omits the existing B/C rows, and `default:` is at lines 1734–1735. The exact historical kill counts remain packet-supplied evidence; I have not opened logs outside the permitted scope.
codex
ASTRA_MTE564F2B_20261018
VERDICT: G1 OUT, G2 OUT, G3 OUT

**Q1.** For **G1 and G2**, the deciding sentence is §4b:

> The rule ENUMERATES joint cells wherever the body distinguishes them; the FLOOR -- the cells a row is OWED for -- is the cross-product of the guard's dominating predicates with the device-STATE lvalues the fix's own repair path reads that are JOINTLY REACHABLE through its own data flow.

**G1: OUT.** I read “the guard’s dominating predicates” as predicates governing arrival at the guard. The counter-reading’s observation that the guard dominates its repaired **body** changes the object being dominated; it does not establish that the guard’s own ACCEPT/REFUSE partition belongs to this bounded product. Section 4a’s broader enumeration does not resolve that omission because §4b expressly distinguishes enumeration from the floor.

The skipped-dispatch amendment incorporates that bound:

> Cross it with the applicable code-tested predicate and state partitions under the existing bounds: the guard's dominating predicates and the jointly reachable device-state lvalues read by the fix's own repair path, as defined by pass 11 above.

Thus it requires the refusal-side label/latch cells, but does not independently require the six disputed ACCEPT/label/SET cells.

**G2: OUT**, under the same §4b sentence. S5’s latch is a repair-path state input; crossing it with S5’s own false outcome requires the same disputed addition to the predicate axes. The surviving legal-mask/SET growth is coverage debt, not a demonstrated breach of the quoted floor.

This is a scope ruling, not a cost argument. **Neither reading makes this enumeration unbounded.** The broader G1 reading gives five labels × two guard outcomes × two latch states: twenty cells per site. G2 gives a finite two-by-two product. Boundedness therefore cannot decide between them; the narrower reading is better supported by the operative wording.

**G3: OUT.** Section 4c explicitly limits the join extension:

> The floor it adds is {labels} x the guard operand's body-tested partition -- six cells there, two rows owed -- never the labels' cross-product with everything.

The larger predicate/state product requires the additional condition:

> a dispatch LABEL is ALSO a floor axis when the edited guard's repair bypasses that dispatch in the enclosing function.

S3–S5 execute inside the selected linear arm. Their returns bypass the remaining fill, not the dispatch. Their `dst_tlb` reads qualify the label/operand product under the join clause, but do not extend it to label/latch joints.

**Q2.** None of the three proposed **OUT dispositions** is wrong on this reading. Explicit disclosure in the probe contract is the correct landing spot, identifying the missing cells and the surviving latch-conditioned growth/exemption classes.

I would tighten the G1/G2 justification: absence of a latch read during ACCEPT is **not independently decisive**. The repair’s read-set still includes the latch. Nor must a product cell appear as an explicit combined conditional. The deciding issue is whether the guard’s own outcome is an authorized floor axis under §4b.

No automatic requirement for multiple rows per cell follows. One adequate row can suffice, with the applicable individual measured kills. Section 4c says:

> Existing rows may discharge them; predictions or aggregate detection do not substitute for the individual measurements.

Shared rows are permissible when their exercised cases and individual measurements establish coverage; fresh or exclusive rows are not inherently required.

